+### 3.3 Request bus
+
+The request bus carries requests from the tiles to the memory system and to C&C: reads and writes of LPDDR, and debug messages. It is separate from the mesh, it has back-pressure, and it never drops anything. Requests travel one way only; every answer comes back over the mesh, through C&C's port (6.7). The tile's side, the request port, is 4.18.
+
+**Topology.** An 8-ary tree with the tiles as leaves:
+- **eight row nodes,** one per row of 8 tiles (tiles 8*r* … 8*r* + 7), each with one input per tile;
+- **a root node,** with one input per row node;
+- **the root queue,** 64 requests deep, at the memory system and C&C, which take requests from it and dispatch each by its target (6.7).
+
+Every tile is a leaf at the same depth, two nodes from the root queue.
+
+**Links.** Every hop is 8 bits wide and moves one byte per cycle, with **ready/valid back-pressure**: a byte moves only when the next stage can take it, so the bus never drops a byte. Long wires are pipelined. *(param)* `reqbus_latency`, the unloaded time from a send store to the request being in the root queue, is about 15–20 cycles [estimate].
+
+**Framing.** A request is a header byte followed by its payload, least significant byte first *(Proposed)*:
+
+| byte | content |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | header: sender [5:0], size [7:6] |
+| 1 … *n* | the payload, bits [7:0] first; *n* = 4, 8 or 16 by size |
+
+- **size** is in the link word's encoding: 00 = 32, 01 = 64, 10 = 128 bits. The request port sets it from the send register used (4.18), so 11 never occurs. **Only 64-bit requests are defined.** 32- and 128-bit requests travel like any other and are room to grow; 6.7 says what the memory system does with them.
+- **sender** is the tile index, stamped by the tree, not by software *(Proposed)*: the tile's port sends 0 there, the row node writes the tile's column (its input index) into [2:0], and the root writes the row (its input index) into [5:3]. A request cannot claim another tile's index, as a packet cannot claim another src.
+
+**Arbitration** *(Proposed)*. Each node has an **8-byte buffer per input** and one output, and is **wormhole-routed per request**. When its output is free, the node picks the next input, round-robin from the one after the input it last served, whose buffer holds a header byte; it then passes that request's bytes, header and payload (the length follows from the size), and only then chooses again. A request's bytes stay together all the way to the root queue.
+- **Fair and starvation-free:** every tile is a leaf at the same depth and every node round-robins over its occupied inputs, so under full load each tile gets exactly 1/64 of the root's requests.
+- **Throughput:** the root passes one byte per cycle, so the whole chip issues at most one 64-bit request (9 bytes) every 9 cycles [derived]. That is ample: a request moves 128 B to 512 KB, and a 4 KB read every 9 cycles would be about 455 B per cycle, far beyond LPDDR [derived].
+- **Ordering:** a tile's requests reach the root queue in the order the tile sent them (one path, FIFO buffers, whole requests). There is no order between tiles.
+
+**Back-pressure end to end.** When the root queue is full the root stalls, the row nodes stall behind it, a tile's port stops draining its request FIFO, and a send store to a full FIFO stalls that tile's controller (4.18). Nothing is dropped anywhere on the bus, and heavy use by one tile only stalls that tile.
+
+**The 64-bit request.** Its fields are below; what the memory system and C&C do with them is 6.7. Sizes and addresses are in **128-byte units**, the minimum transfer and the alignment of every buffer on both sides:
+
+| bits | field | values |
+|---|---|---|
+| [1:0] | target | 0 the memory system, 1 C&C, 2–3 spare |
+| [3:2] | op | 0 read, 1 write, 2 debug message, 3 spare |
+| [5:4] | destination kind | for a read, how its data arrives: 0 big SRAM at an explicit address (mode 9), 1 controller memory (mode 5), 2 the replayer (mode 8), 3 spare. For a write, [4] = 1 requests an acknowledgement and [5] is 0 *(Proposed)* |
+| [9:6] | tag | 0–15: the bit of `WAKE_EVENTS` that the request's answers set (4.12, 6.7), so a tile has at most 16 reads and writes outstanding |
+| [35:10] | LPDDR address | in 128 B units: 2²⁶ × 128 B = 8 GB |
+| [47:36] | count − 1 | in 128 B units: 128 B to 512 KB |
+| [63:48] | tile address | for a read, in 128 B units: 2¹⁶ × 128 B = 8 MB, the whole 21-bit chunk space (6.7 gives each destination kind's mapping); unused for writes and sent as 0 |
+
+**Requests to C&C** (target 1) fix only target and op; bits [63:4] are C&C's software's to define. **A debug message** (op 2) to either target carries a format id and arguments in [63:4], as software defines (6.7).
