+### 2.7 `REQUEST_`: the request port
+
+<!-- generated: reg REQUEST_SET_W0 REQUEST_SET_W1 REQUEST_SET_W2 REQUEST_SEND_32 REQUEST_SEND_64 REQUEST_SEND_128 -->
+<!-- end generated -->
+
+The request port sends requests to the memory system and to C&C over the **request bus**, a byte-wide tree separate from the mesh (spec 3.3, 4.18). It is how a tile reads and writes LPDDR and sends debug messages.
+
+**A send store** pushes one request into the tile's **2 KB request FIFO** (256 entries of 64 bits): `REQUEST_SET_W0` (bits [31:0]), `W1` and `W2` hold the lower words, and the send register stored to supplies the top word and sets the size: `REQUEST_SEND_32` bits [31:0], `REQUEST_SEND_64` bits [63:32], `REQUEST_SEND_128` bits [127:96]. Store the words in ascending order; the last store sends. All three `W` registers then become 0. **Only 64-bit requests are defined:** `REQUEST_SET_W0`, then `REQUEST_SEND_64`.
+
+**Timing.** The store stalls only if the FIFO has no room for the request and no entry leaves in that cycle. `request_busy` (`TILE_STATUS` [11], 2.1.1) says the FIFO is full. There is no acknowledgement for issuing: a request answers, if at all, through its wake event (2.6). The tree stamps the sender; the FIFO keeps draining in HALT.
+
+**The 64-bit request** (spec 3.3; addresses and sizes in 128-byte units, the minimum transfer and every buffer's alignment):
+
+| word | bits | field |
+|---|---|---|
+| `REQUEST_SET_W0` | [1:0] | target: 0 the memory system, 1 C&C |
+| | [3:2] | op: 0 read, 1 write, 2 debug message |
+| | [5:4] | destination kind for a read: 0 big SRAM (mode 9), 1 controller memory (mode 5), 2 the replayer (mode 8); for a write, [4] = 1 asks for an acknowledgement |
+| | [9:6] | tag: the `WAKE_EVENTS` bit its answers set |
+| | [31:10] | LPDDR address [21:0] |
+| `REQUEST_SEND_64` | [3:0] | LPDDR address [25:22] |
+| | [15:4] | count − 1 (128 B to 512 KB) |
+| | [31:16] | tile address (a read's destination; 0 for a write) |
+
+**A read** arrives as 128-bit packets from C&C (src 64), uncounted, at tile address × 32 (a chunk address for big SRAM, a packed word for controller memory, a replayer word address), stepping 4 per packet, then a mode-10 completion sets the tag's event. **It has landed** once the event is set and then `mem_idle` (big SRAM) or `cm_idle` (controller memory) reads 1.
+
+**A write** is token-gated: send the request, wait for the tag's event (the token), consume it, then stream the block to C&C with a `STREAM` entry of *d* = 64, mode 9 and destination word 0 (3.4), so each packet's addr is its chunk offset within the write. With an acknowledgement requested, the tag's event fires again once a later read will see the data.
+
+**Rules** (spec 6.7).
+- At most one read outstanding per replayer queue.
+- No read outstanding and no write token pending across a group run.
+- Read-port results and memory deliveries don't mix (2.4).
+- A controller-memory read stays inside controller memory and below the mailbox: tile address + count ≤ 120.
+- A request the memory system can't serve is dropped and flagged at C&C; the tile waits until C&C's watchdog acts.
